    {
      "blockId": "tour:itinerary_text:173cf987634ed90a",
      "field": "itinerary.text",
      "sourceTextSha256": "374be9926c84dedf1e386521aa40e7ae2ba02669f97adcfc9f221c64b5375ca4",
      "text": "After breakfast, you will head to Cajas National Park, about 30 km west of Cuenca. The park covers 28,544 hectares and contains more than 250 small lakes. Cajas was designated a national park on 5 November 1996.\n\nValleys, lakes, rock formations and the high-altitude páramo ecosystem shape the landscape. The ground is covered in dense grass and cushion plants that retain large quantities of water. Low shrubs also grow in the park.\n\nThe road reaches Tres Cruces, the highest point. It is a continental divide: some rivers flow towards the Pacific Ocean, while others flow towards the Atlantic. The weather in Cajas is often cold, windy and rainy.\n\nThe park is a good place to observe high-altitude plants and birds, including hummingbirds and grey-breasted mountain toucans. There is almost no woodland around the hundreds of lakes. Open páramo, low shrubs and damp ground make for a rugged highland landscape. A boxed lunch is provided.\n\nIn the afternoon, you will travel to Guayaquil, a drive of about 2.5 hours. On arrival, your guides will accompany you to the hotel. The evening is at leisure.\n\n**Accommodation:** Wyndham 4+*.  \n**Meals:** breakfast, boxed lunch.",
      "provenance": {
        "kind": "ai_translation",
        "runId": "ada-en-translator-20261110-mw9trnme-si5iwi",
        "model": "GPT-6.1 Sol"
      }
    },
